# tools/css/check-webref-package.py: the places where the tables (generated from the pinned w3c/webref data) and the @webref/css package
# (tools/css/WEBREF_PACKAGE) do not say the same thing about a name or its syntax. Columns: kind, name, reason (at least twenty characters,
# and true: each reason says what was looked at). A difference that is not here fails, and so does a row for a difference that is gone.
#
# Two kinds of difference are here.
#
# * type: the package lists a type or function that is *nested inside another type's entry or a descriptor's entry* of the pinned files
#   (their `values` array: `<basic-shape>` holds `inset()` and `xywh()`; `<color>` holds `<alpha-value>`; `size` of @page holds
#   `<page-size>`; `shape()` holds its command types). tools/css/webref_model.py reads the nested definitions of a *property* only, so the
#   tables have none of these. This is a gap in the generator that this check found, not a choice: where the library does not define the
#   name itself (as it does for the basic-shape functions and repeat()), a grammar that names it does not link and is listed in
#   tools/css/UNRESOLVED_REFERENCES. Reading them needs a scope that is a type, not only a property (repeat() is defined three times, in three types), and is its own piece of work.
# * type-syntax: both have the name and the texts differ; the reason gives both and says which side is the older reading.
#
